"Chinese landscape painters thought about distance too. In the eleventh century the painter Guo Xi described three kinds of distance in a mountain scene: looking up from the foot of a mountain to its peak, looking from the front of a mountain to what lies behind, and looking from a near mountain across to far ones. All these painters, east and west, were solving the same puzzle. The paper is flat, and the world is not.": "66a0b1575ca0",
"Draw five trees in a row, all the same size, all the same green, all standing on one line. Your picture is flat. Nothing in it says which tree is close and which is far. Yet a landscape painter can make you feel that one hill is a short walk away and another is a day's journey. Painters do it with a small set of cues, and you already read every one of them without thinking.": "421e498fc06b",
"Draw five trees in a row, all the same size, all the same green, all standing on one line. Your picture is flat. Nothing in it says which tree is close and which is far. Yet a landscape painter can make you feel that one hill is a short walk away and another is a day's journey. Painters do it with a small set of cues, and you already read every one of them without thinking. The first cue is overlap. If one tree hides part of another, the hidden one must be behind. Overlap tells you the order of things, though it cannot tell you how far apart they are. The second cue is size. When you know two trees are about the same size, the one that looks smaller seems farther away. This is the rule you met in Lesson 5: twice as far, half as tall. The third cue is position on the picture. Look at a field or a floor. The ground near your feet is at the bottom of your view, and the ground far away is higher up, close to the horizon. So a tree whose foot is drawn higher on the picture looks farther off. This works for things on the ground, below your eye level. Clouds above you do the opposite. The fourth cue is the air itself. Air scatters sunlight, and that scattered light is added to everything you see through it. The more air lies between you and a hill, the paler the hill looks, and the less contrast it has. Scattered sky light usually has more blue in it, so far hills usually look bluish. Artists call this aerial perspective, or atmospheric perspective. Leonardo da Vinci wrote about it in his notebooks about five hundred years ago. He called it aerial perspective, because, he said, the atmosphere lets us tell how far away different buildings are. His advice to painters was plain. Paint the nearest building in its real color, and make the more distant ones less defined and bluer. Chinese landscape painters thought about distance too. In the eleventh century the painter Guo Xi described three kinds of distance in a mountain scene: looking up from the foot of a mountain to its peak, looking from the front of a mountain to what lies behind, and looking from a near mountain across to far ones. All these painters, east and west, were solving the same puzzle. The paper is flat, and the world is not.": "b68e32c03850",
